q(74, "The shared MOA of class II and class IV drugs in the table is:", ["Relative AV nodal block", "Na+-K+ ATPase blockade", "Direct renin inhibition", "Aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibition"], "The MOA row says relative AV nodal block individually.")
q(74, "Class II β blockers are used for:", ["SVT/PSVT", "Only long-QT pacing", "Only BPH", "Only renal artery stenosis"], "The class II uses list begins with SVT/PSVT.")
q(74, "β blockers are DOC for idiopathic ventricular tachycardia because they decrease:", ["Heart rate", "Aqueous humour outflow", "Uric acid excretion", "Accessory pathway refractoriness only"], "The table says idiopathic VT: β blocker → ↓ HR.")
q(74, "Another class II use listed is:", ["Ventricular premature beats", "Pulmonary fibrosis", "Monday disease", "Hyperreninemic hypertension"], "Ventricular premature beats are listed under class II uses.")
q(74, "β blockers treat catecholamine-induced arrhythmia due to:", ["Pheochromocytoma", "BPH", "Renal artery stenosis", "Nitrate tolerance"], "Pheochromocytoma is listed under catecholamine-induced arrhythmia.")
q(74, "Halothane can cause catecholamine-induced arrhythmia because it:", ["Sensitizes myocardium to catecholamine", "Blocks AV node totally alone", "Inhibits neprilysin", "Opens Na+-iodide symporter"], "The table notes halothane sensitizes myocardium to catecholamine.")
q(74, "Class IV calcium channel blockers are used in:", ["SVT/PSVT", "HOCM surgery only", "Insulinoma", "Scorpion bite"], "The class IV use column says SVT/PSVT.")
q(74, "Class II and class IV drugs are contraindicated with each other because combining them can cause:", ["Total conduction block", "Reflex tachycardia", "Xanthopsia", "Hyperuricemia"], "The note says class II and IV are C/I with each other, never combined: total conduction block.")

unit("Class III K+ Channel Blockers and Amiodarone Spectrum",
     "Class III drugs delay ventricular repolarization, producing QT prolongation and torsades risk. Ibutilide has the maximum torsades risk, whereas amiodarone has minimum torsades risk because it blocks K+, Na+, β and Ca2+ channels. Amiodarone is the broadest and longest-acting antiarrhythmic; ibutilide is the shortest-acting K+ blocker used IV for acute fibrillation/flutter attacks.")
q(74, "Class III K+ channel blockers delay which phase in ventricular cells?", ["Repolarisation", "Depolarisation only", "Digitalization", "Renal filtration"], "The class III MOA says ventricular cell: delay in repolarisation.")
q(74, "Delayed repolarisation from class III drugs can lead to:", ["QT prolongation and torsades de pointes", "QT shortening only", "PR shortening only", "No ECG effect"], "The class III MOA says can lead to QT prolongation → torsades de pointes.")
q(74, "Torsades risk among class III drugs is maximum with:", ["Ibutilide", "Amiodarone", "Sotalol", "Bretylium"], "The risk bracket says maximum: ibutilide.")
q(74, "Torsades risk among class III drugs is minimum with:", ["Amiodarone", "Ibutilide", "Dofetilide", "Vernakalant"], "The risk bracket says minimum: amiodarone.")
q(74, "Amiodarone is described as the:", ["Most wide-spectrum antiarrhythmic", "Shortest-acting antiarrhythmic", "Only class IV drug", "Pure adenosine receptor agonist"], "The amiodarone MOA line says most wide spectrum antiarrhythmic.")
