q(75, "A loading dose of amiodarone is used to:", ["Achieve steady state concentration faster", "Prevent all iodine toxicity", "Block renal excretion", "Avoid any need for monitoring"], "The note says use loading dose → achieve steady state concentration faster.")

unit("Amiodarone Adverse Effects and Dronedarone",
     "Amiodarone toxicity is dominated by iodine-related lung, liver, skin, thyroid and ocular problems. Pulmonary fibrosis needs baseline X-ray/PFT and is contraindicated in ILD; corneal deposits are whorl-like, usually asymptomatic and reversible; skin effects include ceruloderma and brown photosensitive pigmentation; and thyroid disease is more often hypothyroid in plain dwellers than hyperthyroid in hilly areas. Dronedarone lacks iodine, so it is less toxic but also less efficacious.")
q(75, "Amiodarone pulmonary toxicity listed is:", ["Pulmonary fibrosis", "Bronchial asthma from β2 block", "Nasolacrimal obstruction", "Pulmonary hypertension only"], "The first side effect says pulmonary fibrosis.")
q(75, "Before starting amiodarone, the page advises X-ray/PFT especially because it is contraindicated in:", ["Interstitial lung disease", "BPH", "Gout", "WPW"], "The pulmonary fibrosis line says C/I in ILD: do X-ray/PFT prior to initiating drug.")
q(75, "Amiodarone corneal deposits are described as:", ["Whorl-like", "Cherry-red", "Cotton-wool", "Kayser-Fleischer only"], "The note under the eye image says whorl-like corneal deposits.")
q(75, "Amiodarone corneal deposits are usually:", ["Asymptomatic", "Always blinding", "Always nephrotoxic", "Always painful"], "The corneal-deposit bullet says usually asymptomatic.")
q(75, "If amiodarone corneal deposits become symptomatic, the instruction is to:", ["Change the drug", "Increase the dose", "Add digoxin", "Perform renal angiography"], "The note says if symptomatic → change drug.")
q(75, "Corneal deposits from amiodarone are reversible because deposited drug is reabsorbed after:", ["Stopping the drug", "Adding iodine", "Adding digoxin", "Defibrillation"], "The reversible note says deposited drug reabsorbed into circulation on stopping drug.")
q(75, "Whorl-like corneal deposits are also seen with chloroquine/chlorpromazine and rarely with:", ["Indomethacin", "Ibuprofen", "Aspirin", "Paracetamol"], "The note lists chloroquine/chlorpromazine and indomethacin rarely.")
q(75, "Blue-grey pigmentation with amiodarone is called:", ["Ceruloderma", "Xanthopsia", "Sweet syndrome", "Monday disease"], "The pigmentation line labels blue/grey pigmentation as ceruloderma.")
q(75, "Ceruloderma from amiodarone is due to lipofuscin deposition in:", ["Sun-exposed areas", "Renal tubules", "Prostatic urethra", "Pulmonary capillaries only"], "The note says lipofuscin deposition in sun exposed areas.")
q(75, "Which cardiac inflammation is listed as an amiodarone adverse effect?", ["Myocarditis", "Endocardial cushion defect", "Pericardial tamponade only", "Atrial septal defect"], "Myocarditis is side effect number 4.")
