q(76, "Initial non-drug management before adenosine in acute SVT/PSVT is:", ["Carotid massage", "Renal angiography", "Digitalization", "Sunscreen"], "The use note says initial mx: carotid massage.")
q(76, "Carotid massage works by vagal stimulation causing:", ["AV block", "Accessory pathway opening", "Renin release", "Bronchodilation"], "The carotid massage flow says vagal stimulation → AV block.")
q(76, "Adenosine can maintain controlled hypotension during surgery due to:", ["Vasodilation", "Bronchoconstriction", "Aldosterone release", "Na+ channel blockade"], "The use list says maintain controlled hypotension d/t vasodilatation, e.g. bleeding during surgery.")
q(76, "Adenosine is the shortest-acting antiarrhythmic, with half-life:", ["1-5 seconds", "53 days", "36-48 hours", "7-10 days"], "The T1/2 note says shortest acting antiarrhythmic (1-5 sec).")

unit("Adenosine Adverse Effects, Digoxin and Magnesium Sulfate",
     "Adenosine commonly causes transient apnea and flushing; it may trigger atrial fibrillation by K+ channel opening and is contraindicated in asthma/COPD, atrial fibrillation and transplanted hearts because of denervation hypersensitivity. Digoxin blocks the AV node and is used as a chronic CHF inotrope plus SVT/PSVT drug. MgSO4 blocks Ca2+ channels to shorten QT and is DOC for acute torsades; congenital long QT is managed with β blockers and pacing, while acquired drug-induced long QT requires avoiding drugs.")
