unit("Chronic CHF Compensation, Mortality and Oral Drug Classes",
     "Chronic CHF follows gradual loss of output from cardiomyopathy/rheumatic disease, then compensatory catecholamines and RAAS. Compensation may maintain output but drives cardiac remodeling, oxygen demand and mortality; decompensation resembles acute CHF and lowers oxygen supply. Oral mortality-reducing drugs target β1 signaling, heart rate, vasodilation, RAAS and sodium/water retention, while furosemide and digoxin remain symptomatic drugs.")
q(78, "Chronic CHF pathophysiology starts with cardiomyopathy or:", ["Rheumatic heart disease", "Myocardial infarction as only cause", "BPH", "Insulinoma"], "The chronic CHF flow begins cardiomyopathy/rheumatic heart disease.")
q(78, "The first hemodynamic change in chronic CHF is gradual decrease in:", ["Cardiac output", "Renin always", "Oxygen demand", "Aldosterone always"], "The flow says gradual ↓ in cardiac output.")
q(78, "Gradual fall in cardiac output activates:", ["Compensatory mechanisms", "Only corneal deposition", "Aldehyde dehydrogenase", "PDE5 inhibitors"], "The flow says activate compensatory mechanisms.")
q(78, "Decompensated chronic CHF has features similar to:", ["Acute CHF", "BPH", "Glaucoma", "Raynaud disease"], "The left branch says decompensated chronic CHF → features similar to acute CHF.")
q(78, "Decompensated chronic CHF increases mortality partly by decreasing:", ["Oxygen supply", "Oxygen demand", "Renin", "Catecholamine release"], "The left branch leads to ↓ O2 supply → ↑ mortality.")
q(78, "Compensated chronic CHF is driven by increased release of:", ["Catecholamines", "Only BNP breakdown products", "Only acetylcholine", "Only iodine"], "The compensated branch says ↑ release of catecholamines.")
q(78, "The catecholamines listed in compensated chronic CHF are:", ["Epinephrine, norepinephrine and dopamine", "Serotonin, histamine and bradykinin", "Insulin, glucagon and aldosterone", "Adenosine, atropine and digoxin"], "The flow lists epinephrine, norepinephrine and dopamine.")
q(78, "Catecholamine α1 stimulation in chronic CHF causes:", ["Vasoconstriction", "Vasodilation", "Diuresis", "AV node block"], "The α1 branch says vasoconstriction.")
q(78, "Catecholamine β1 stimulation in the heart increases:", ["Contraction and heart rate", "Aqueous humour and IOP", "Uric acid and HDL", "Renal GFR only"], "The β1 heart branch lists ↑ contraction and ↑ HR.")
q(78, "Catecholamine β1 stimulation in the kidney activates:", ["RAAS", "PDE5", "AChE", "Na+ channel blockade"], "The β1 kidney branch says RAAS activation.")
q(78, "RAAS activation in chronic CHF causes angiotensin-mediated:", ["Vasoconstriction", "Bronchodilation", "K+ retention only", "Aqueous humour fall only"], "The kidney branch lists angiotensin: vasoconstriction.")
q(78, "Aldosterone in chronic CHF causes retention of:", ["Na+ and H2O", "K+ only", "Uric acid only", "Iodide only"], "The RAAS branch says aldosterone: Na+ + H2O retention.")
q(78, "Compensated chronic CHF causes cardiac remodeling, defined here as:", ["Hypertrophy of cardiac muscle", "Apoptosis of renal JG cells", "Atrial stasis only", "Floppy iris"], "The flow labels cardiac remodeling as hypertrophy of cardiac muscle.")
q(78, "Cardiac remodeling increases mortality by increasing:", ["Oxygen demand", "Oxygen supply", "Aqueous outflow", "LDL clearance"], "The compensated branch leads to ↑ O2 demand → ↑ mortality.")
q(78, "Decompensated chronic CHF and acute CHF presenting similarly is called:", ["Acute heart failure syndrome", "WPW syndrome", "Monday disease", "Ceruloderma"], "The note brackets decompensated chronic CHF and acute CHF as acute heart failure syndrome.")
q(78, "The route of administration in chronic CHF management is:", ["Oral", "IV only", "Sublingual only", "Nebulised only"], "The chronic CHF management section says route of administration: oral.")
q(78, "The best β blockers listed to decrease mortality in chronic CHF are:", ["Carvedilol, metoprolol, bisoprolol and nebivolol", "Propranolol, timolol, nadolol and pindolol", "Tamsulosin, silodosin, prazosin and doxazosin", "Quinidine, procainamide, lidocaine and flecainide"], "The mortality table lists carvedilol, metoprolol, bisoprolol and nebivolol.")
q(78, "β blockers reduce mortality in chronic CHF by inhibiting β1 in the heart, lowering:", ["Contraction and heart rate", "BNP and cGMP", "Uric acid and LDL", "Aldosterone breakdown"], "The β-blocker MOA cell says inhibit β1 in heart → ↓ contraction, ↓ HR.")
q(78, "Ivabradine decreases mortality by decreasing:", ["Heart rate", "Afterload through α1 block", "Na+-K+ ATPase", "Neprilysin"], "The ivabradine MOA row says ↓ heart rate.")
q(78, "Isosorbide dinitrate plus hydralazine reduces mortality by:", ["Vasodilation", "Increasing renin", "Blocking AV node only", "Raising preload"], "The table lists vasodilation as its MOA.")
q(78, "Vericiguat in chronic CHF produces:", ["Vasodilation", "Na+ channel blockade", "β3 bladder relaxation", "AChE inhibition"], "Vericiguat shares the vasodilation MOA cell in the table.")
q(78, "ACE inhibitors, ARBs, spironolactone and sacubitril reduce mortality by inhibiting:", ["RAAS", "PDE5 only", "Adenosine receptors", "Na+ channels"], "The table groups these drugs with MOA inhibit RAAS.")
q(78, "The SGLT-2 blocker listed in CHF is:", ["Canagliflozin", "Sotagliflozin", "Dapagliflozin only", "Empagliflozin only"], "The table explicitly lists SGLT-2 blockers: canagliflozin.")
q(78, "The SGLT-1/2 blocker listed in CHF is:", ["Sotagliflozin", "Canagliflozin", "Vericiguat", "Ivabradine"], "The table lists SGLT-1/2 blockers: sotagliflozin.")
q(78, "SGLT blockers reduce mortality by decreasing retention of:", ["Na+ and H2O", "K+ only", "Ca2+ only", "Iodine"], "The table MOA says ↓ Na+ + H2O retention.")
q(78, "Symptomatic drugs in chronic CHF include:", ["Furosemide and digoxin", "Flecainide and adenosine", "Prazosin and silodosin", "Sildenafil and tadalafil"], "The symptomatic management list names furosemide and digoxin.")
q(78, "Furosemide helps symptomatic CHF by reducing:", ["Volume overload", "Renin testing error", "Floppy iris", "Corneal deposits"], "The symptomatic list says furosemide: ↓ volume overload.")

unit("Newly Diagnosed CHF Approach and Neprilysin/Guanylate Cyclase Drugs",
     "A newly diagnosed CHF patient first receives furosemide for symptoms, then a mortality-reducing foundation: sacubitril+valsartan if affordable is preferred to ACEI/ARB, otherwise ACEI/ARB are started low and titrated. β blockers are started while ACEI/ARB are being increased, beginning at suboptimal dose to prevent decompensation. Sacubitril raises BNP by inhibiting neprilysin, vericiguat raises cGMP through guanylate cyclase, and omapatrilat is obsolete because dual NEP+ACE inhibition caused fatal angioedema.")
q(79, "The initial drug in a newly diagnosed CHF case is:", ["Furosemide", "Digoxin", "Flecainide", "Adenosine"], "The Rx approach says initial drug: furosemide.")
q(79, "The preferred affordable initial mortality-reducing drug pair is:", ["Sacubitril plus valsartan", "Digoxin plus atropine", "Sildenafil plus tadalafil", "Prazosin plus terazosin"], "The approach says sacubitril + valsartan is preferred if affordable.")
q(79, "Sacubitril plus valsartan is preferred over:", ["ACE inhibitor or ARB", "Digoxin and furosemide", "Adenosine and MgSO4", "β blocker monotherapy only"], "The comparison shows sacubitril+valsartan > ACE inhibitor/ARB.")
q(79, "Sacubitril and valsartan are fused at the:", ["Molecular level", "Nuclear level", "Renal artery level", "Atrial node level"], "The note says fused at molecular level.")
q(79, "ACEI/ARB therapy in new CHF starts at low/suboptimal dose and is gradually increased to:", ["Maximum tolerable dose", "Toxic dose", "No dose change ever", "Only IV dose"], "The note says gradually increased to maximum tolerable dose.")
q(79, "Increasing ACEI/ARB can raise renin and vasodilation, creating risk of:", ["Postural hypotension", "Xanthopsia", "Floppy iris", "Agranulocytosis only"], "The ACEI/ARB note says ↑ renin → ↑ vasodilation → risk of postural hypotension.")
q(79, "β blockers in CHF are started while increasing ACEI/ARB toward:", ["Maximum tolerable dose", "Zero dose", "Immediate toxic dose", "Only topical dose"], "The β-blocker start note says while increasing ACE-I/ARB to maximum tolerable dose.")
q(79, "β blockers in CHF are begun at suboptimal dose and gradually increased to prevent:", ["Decompensation", "Floppy iris", "Nitrate tolerance", "Torsades"], "The page says at suboptimal dose and gradually increase to prevent decompensation.")
q(79, "If the first CHF steps give no response, the page says to:", ["Add other drugs", "Stop all therapy", "Use only adenosine", "Perform defibrillation"], "The flow below β blockers says if no response → add other drugs.")
q(79, "Sacubitril inhibits:", ["NEP/neprilysin", "ACE only", "PDE5 only", "Na+-K+ ATPase"], "The drug map says sacubitril MOA: inhibits NEP/neprilysin.")
q(79, "By inhibiting neprilysin, sacubitril increases:", ["BNP", "Aldosterone", "Uric acid", "Aqueous humour"], "The flow under sacubitril says ↑ BNP.")
q(79, "BNP stands for:", ["Brain natriuretic peptide", "Bronchial nitric peptide", "Beta nodal protein", "Blood nephron peptide"], "The page expands BNP as Brain Natriuretic Peptide.")
q(79, "Sacubitril can cause angioedema and is contraindicated with:", ["ACE inhibitors", "β blockers", "Furosemide", "Digoxin"], "The side-effect note says angioedema, C/I with ACE-I.")
q(79, "Vericiguat is a:", ["Guanylate cyclase stimulator", "β blocker", "Direct renin inhibitor", "Na+ channel blocker"], "The vericiguat MOA says guanylate cyclase stimulator.")
q(79, "Vericiguat increases:", ["cGMP", "cAMP only", "LDL", "Renin"], "The vericiguat flow says ↑ cGMP.")
q(79, "The final effect of vericiguat in CHF is:", ["Vasodilation", "Bronchoconstriction", "Atrial thrombosis", "Na+ channel opening"], "The vericiguat pathway ends with vasodilation.")
q(79, "Omapatrilat is obsolete because it caused severe:", ["Angioedema", "Xanthopsia", "Hirsutism", "Floppy iris"], "The note says omapatrilat is obsolete causing severe angioedema.")
q(79, "Death from omapatrilat angioedema occurs due to:", ["Laryngeal edema", "Renal edema", "Ankle edema", "Corneal edema"], "The note says death due to laryngeal edema.")
q(79, "Omapatrilat inhibits:", ["NEP and ACE", "PDE5 and COMT", "Na+ and K+ channels", "β1 and β2 receptors"], "The MOA note calls it a vasopeptidase inhibitor inhibiting NEP and ACE.")

unit("Digoxin Pharmacokinetics and Therapeutic Concentrations",
     "Digoxin comes from Digitalis lanata, has good oral absorption, a long 36-48 hour half-life and renal excretion. Digitalization takes 7-10 days, so therapeutic drug monitoring should begin after that to avoid false low values. The normal plasma range is 0.5-2.0 ng/mL, but the desired therapeutic range is 0.5-0.9 ng/mL; toxicity rises above 2.0 ng/mL and mortality in atrial fibrillation rises above 1.2 ng/mL.")
q(79, "Digoxin is derived from:", ["Digitalis lanata", "Rauwolfia serpentina", "Atropa belladonna", "Papaver somniferum"], "The source line says Digitalis lanata.")
q(79, "Digitalis lanata is also called:", ["White foxglove plant", "Red cinchona bark", "Purple periwinkle", "Green tea leaf"], "The page labels Digitalis lanata as white foxglove plant.")
q(79, "Digoxin has:", ["Good oral absorption", "No oral absorption", "Only inhalational absorption", "Only topical absorption"], "The pharmacokinetics section says good oral absorption.")
q(79, "Digoxin half-life is about:", ["36-48 hours", "1-5 seconds", "53 days", "Less than 1 second"], "The distribution line gives T1/2: 36-48 hours.")
q(79, "Time for digitalization to achieve steady-state plasma concentration is:", ["7-10 days", "1-5 seconds", "53 days", "Less than 1 hour"], "The page says digitalization takes 7-10 days.")
q(79, "Therapeutic drug monitoring for digoxin is started after digitalization to avoid:", ["False low values", "False high glucose", "False low renin", "False positive captopril test"], "The TDM note says start after 7-10 days to avoid false low values.")
q(79, "Normal plasma concentration of digoxin is:", ["0.5-2.0 ng/mL", "0.5-0.9 ng/mL", "2.0-5.0 ng/mL", "6-12 mg"], "The line says normal plasma concentration: 0.5-2.0 ng/ml.")
q(79, "Therapeutic plasma concentration of digoxin is:", ["0.5-0.9 ng/mL", "0.5-2.0 ng/mL", "2.0-3.0 ng/mL", "36-48 ng/mL"], "The line says therapeutic plasma concentration: 0.5-0.9 ng/ml.")
q(80, "There is no added advantage when digoxin dose exceeds:", ["0.9 ng/mL", "0.5 ng/mL", "1.2 ng/mL", "2.0 ng/mL"], "The note says no added advantage if dose >0.9 ng/ml.")
q(80, "Digoxin toxicity is classically above:", ["2.0 ng/mL", "0.9 ng/mL", "0.5 ng/mL", "0.1 ng/mL"], "The toxicity bullet says >2.0 ng/ml.")
q(80, "In atrial fibrillation, digoxin levels above which value increase mortality risk?", ["1.2 ng/mL", "0.5 ng/mL", "0.9 ng/mL", "0.1 ng/mL"], "The toxicity note says >1.2 ng/ml: ↑ risk of mortality in atrial fibrillation.")
q(80, "Digoxin is excreted by the:", ["Kidney", "Lung", "Skin", "Bile only"], "The excretion line says renal.")
q(80, "Dose variation with digoxin can occur due to electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalemia and:", ["Hypercalcemia", "Hypocalcemia only", "Hyponatremia only", "Hypernatremia only"], "The note lists hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, etc.")

unit("Digoxin Cellular Mechanism and Ventricular Arrhythmias",
     "At the cellular level, digoxin blocks the Na+-K+ ATPase pump. This raises intracellular Na+, blocks the Na+-Ca2+ exchanger, raises intracellular Ca2+ and increases myocardial contraction; extracellular K+ competes with digoxin binding, while diuretic-induced hypokalemia leaves more ATPase available and precipitates toxicity. In ventricles, delayed closure of Ca2+ channels causes DADs, extrasystoles, bigeminy, trigeminy, VT and finally VF, where defibrillation should be avoided.")
q(80, "Digoxin directly blocks the:", ["Na+-K+ ATPase pump", "PDE5 enzyme", "ACE enzyme", "Adenosine uptake protein"], "The mechanism line says digoxin blocks Na+-K+ ATPase pump.")
q(80, "K+ occupancy of Na+-K+ ATPase causes:", ["Decreased digoxin binding", "Increased digoxin binding", "No effect on digoxin", "Immediate AV block only"], "The diagram says K+ occupies Na+-K+ ATPase → ↓ digoxin binding.")
q(80, "By blocking Na+-K+ ATPase, digoxin increases intracellular:", ["Na+", "K+ only", "Iodide", "cGMP"], "The right branch says ↑ intracellular Na+.")
q(80, "Increased intracellular Na+ blocks the:", ["Na+-Ca2+ exchanger", "Na+-iodide symporter", "PDE3 enzyme", "ACE enzyme"], "The mechanism flow says block Na+-Ca2+ exchanger (antiporter).")
q(80, "Blocking the Na+-Ca2+ exchanger increases intracellular:", ["Ca2+", "K+ only", "Iodide", "Uric acid"], "The flow says ↑ intracellular Ca2+.")
q(80, "The final inotropic effect of digoxin is increased:", ["Contraction of myocardium", "Bronchial relaxation", "Renin release", "Uveoscleral outflow"], "The pathway ends with ↑ contraction of myocardium.")
q(80, "Digoxin blockade also increases extracellular K+, producing:", ["Hyperkalemia", "Hypokalemia", "Hyponatremia", "Hypocalcemia"], "The left branch says ↑ extracellular K+ (hyperkalemia).")
q(80, "Hyperkalemia limits digoxin binding to:", ["Na+-K+ ATPase", "PDE5", "Guanylate cyclase", "ACE"], "The left branch says hyperkalemia limits binding of digoxin to Na+-K+ ATPase.")
q(80, "Diuretic-induced hypokalemia increases availability of Na+-K+ ATPase for binding and causes:", ["Digoxin toxicity", "Digoxin antidote effect", "Reduced toxicity", "No digoxin effect"], "The note says diuretics → hypokalemia → ↑ availability of Na+-K+ ATPase for binding → digoxin toxicity.")
q(80, "During repolarization, the page notes opening of K+ channels and closing of:", ["Ca2+ channels", "Na+-K+ ATPase", "ACE", "PDE5"], "The diagram says repolarization → opening of K+ channel + closing of Ca2+ channel.")
q(80, "Delayed afterdepolarization from digoxin is due to delayed closure of:", ["Ca2+ channels", "K+ channels", "ACE", "Renin"], "The ventricular myocardium note says DAD arrhythmia due to delayed closure of Ca2+ channel.")
q(80, "The most common digoxin-induced arrhythmia is:", ["Ventricular bigeminy", "Atrial flutter", "Mobitz type II block", "Sinus bradycardia only"], "The DAD flow marks ventricular bigeminy as m/c digoxin-induced arrhythmia.")
q(80, "Extrasystoles from digoxin can manifest as ventricular bigeminy or:", ["Ventricular trigeminy", "Atrial flutter only", "WPW syndrome", "Long-QT pacing"], "The extrasystole branch lists ventricular bigeminy and ventricular trigeminy.")
q(80, "With further increase in digoxin, ventricular tachycardia may become self-sustained, meaning:", ["Every cell produces action potential", "No cell produces action potential", "Only SA node fires", "Only AV node blocks"], "The VT branch says self-sustained (every cell produces action potential).")
q(80, "Digoxin-induced ventricular tachycardia may be bidirectional due to:", ["Anterograde/retrograde propagation of action potential", "Only atrial stasis", "Only renin release", "Only α1 blockade"], "The VT note says bidirectional (antero/retrograde propagation of action potential).")
q(80, "In digoxin-induced ventricular fibrillation, the page says to avoid:", ["Defibrillation", "Digibind", "Lidocaine", "Amiodarone"], "The final line says ventricular fibrillation: avoid defibrillation.")

unit("Digoxin Toxicity Treatment and Organ-Level Heart Effects",
     "Digoxin toxicity is treated according to rhythm: lidocaine or amiodarone for VT/VF, Digibind as the antidote, and no dialysis because distribution volume is high. At normal dose digoxin is parasympathomimetic with SA/AV nodal block but also raises atrial Ca2+, causing atrial tachycardia as the most specific arrhythmia; therapeutically it converts atrial flutter into atrial fibrillation, which is easier to control. At high dose it becomes sympathomimetic with increased heart rate and contraction.")
q(81, "Digoxin-toxic ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation is treated with:", ["Lidocaine or amiodarone", "Adenosine or atropine", "Prazosin or terazosin", "Sildenafil or tadalafil"], "The Rx list says lidocaine/amiodarone: Rx of V Tach or V Fib.")
q(81, "The antidote for digoxin toxicity is:", ["Digibind", "Glucagon", "Protamine", "Atropine"], "The toxicity treatment list says antidote: Digibind.")
q(81, "Dialysis is not done in digoxin toxicity because digoxin has:", ["High volume of distribution", "Zero tissue distribution", "Only intravascular distribution", "Very short half-life"], "The treatment list says dialysis not done due to high volume of distribution.")
q(81, "At normal dose, digoxin has which autonomic effect on the SA and AV nodes?", ["Parasympathomimetic nodal block", "Pure sympathomimetic stimulation", "β3 agonism", "α1 blockade"], "The organ-level section says at normal dose: parasympathomimetic SA nodal and AV nodal block.")
q(81, "At normal dose, increased Ca2+ in atrial myocardial cells can cause:", ["Atrial tachycardia", "Ventricular standstill only", "HOCM cure", "Pulmonary edema"], "The right branch says ↑ Ca2+ in atrial myocardial cell: atrial tachycardia.")
q(81, "The most specific digoxin-induced arrhythmia is:", ["Atrial tachycardia", "Ventricular bigeminy", "Atrial flutter", "Mobitz type II block"], "The diagram labels atrial tachycardia as the most specific digoxin induced arrhythmia.")
q(81, "Therapeutically, digoxin converts atrial flutter into atrial fibrillation because AF is:", ["Easier to control ventricular rate", "Always fatal", "Impossible to anticoagulate", "Only a ventricular arrhythmia"], "The therapeutic-use line says atrial flutter → atrial fibrillation (easier to control ventricular rate).")
q(81, "At high dose, digoxin becomes:", ["Sympathomimetic", "Pure parasympatholytic", "Pure β blocker", "Direct renin inhibitor"], "The high-dose line says sympathomimetic.")
q(81, "High-dose digoxin increases:", ["Heart rate and contraction", "Only AV block", "Only aqueous outflow", "Only uveoscleral flow"], "The high-dose note says ↑ heart rate, ↑ contraction.")

unit("Digoxin Adverse Effects, Contraindications and DIGOXIN Summary",
     "The classic toxicity cluster is summarized by DIGOXIN: dialysis not done/Digibind, increased K+, gynecomastia, ocular effects/xanthopsia, increased arrhythmias, nausea-vomiting and Na+-K+ ATPase blockade. Nausea/vomiting is both earliest and most common. Toxicity risk rises with hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, MI and renal failure; digoxin is also contraindicated in HOCM and WPW.")
q(81, "The most common and earliest side effect of digoxin is:", ["Nausea and vomiting", "Xanthopsia", "Gynecomastia", "Hyperkalemia"], "The side-effect list says nausea and vomiting: m/c and earliest.")
q(81, "Xanthopsia means:", ["Yellow vision", "Blue-grey skin", "Whorl cornea", "Floppy iris"], "The side-effect list writes xanthopsia (yellow vision).")
q(81, "Digoxin can cause which endocrine side effect?", ["Gynecomastia", "Hirsutism in females", "Hypothyroidism only", "Cushing syndrome"], "Gynecomastia appears in the side-effect list.")
q(81, "Digoxin can cause which electrolyte side effect?", ["Hyperkalemia", "Hypokalemia", "Hyponatremia", "Hypocalcemia"], "Hyperkalemia is in the side-effect list.")
q(81, "Digoxin causes arrhythmias of all types except atrial flutter and:", ["Mobitz type II block", "Ventricular tachycardia", "Atrial tachycardia", "Ventricular bigeminy"], "The arrhythmia side-effect line says all types except atrial flutter and Mobitz type II block.")
q(81, "Which condition increases risk of digoxin toxicity by increasing Ca2+?", ["Hypercalcemia", "Hypocalcemia", "Hyponatremia", "Low BNP"], "Hypercalcemia is listed under conditions increasing risk.")
q(81, "Which potassium disturbance increases digoxin toxicity risk?", ["Hypokalemia", "Hyperkalemia", "Normokalemia", "Potassium excess only protective"], "Hypokalemia appears in the contraindication/risk list.")
q(81, "Which magnesium disturbance increases digoxin toxicity risk?", ["Hypomagnesemia", "Hypermagnesemia", "Normal magnesium", "Only high MgSO4"], "Hypomagnesemia is listed under toxicity risk conditions.")
q(81, "Myocardial infarction increases digoxin toxicity risk because it increases permeability for:", ["Ca2+ ions", "Iodide", "Uric acid", "Aldosterone"], "The MI item says ↑ permeability for Ca2+ ions.")
q(81, "Renal failure increases digoxin toxicity because it decreases:", ["Excretion", "Absorption", "Protein binding only", "Atrial calcium"], "The renal failure item says ↓ excretion.")
q(81, "Digoxin is contraindicated in HOCM because increased contraction causes:", ["Worsening", "Cure", "No change", "Less LV obstruction"], "The contraindication line says HOCM: ↑ contraction → worsening.")
q(81, "Digoxin is contraindicated in WPW syndrome because AV nodal block increases conduction through the:", ["Accessory pathway", "Renal artery", "Pulmonary capillary", "Prostatic urethra"], "The WPW line says blocks AV node → ↑ conduction through accessory pathway.")
q(82, "In the DIGOXIN summary, D stands for dialysis not done and:", ["Digibind antidote", "Diltiazem antidote", "Dobutamine antidote", "Dopamine antidote"], "The summary says D → dialysis not done; Digibind (antidote).")
q(82, "In the DIGOXIN summary, I stands for:", ["Increase K+", "Increase renin", "Increase iodine", "Increase HDL"], "The summary says I → Increase K+.")
q(82, "In the DIGOXIN summary, G stands for:", ["Gynecomastia", "Glaucoma", "Gout", "Glucagon"], "The summary says G → gynecomastia.")
q(82, "In the DIGOXIN summary, O stands for:", ["Ocular side effects", "Oral absorption failure", "Obesity only", "Omapatrilat"], "The summary says O → ocular S/E.")
q(82, "In the DIGOXIN summary, X stands for:", ["Xanthopsia", "Xerostomia", "Xylometazoline", "Xanthine oxidase"], "The summary says X → xanthopsia.")
q(82, "In the DIGOXIN summary, the final N stands for nausea/vomiting and:", ["Na+-K+ ATPase blocker", "Nitric oxide donor", "Neprilysin activator", "Nifedipine"], "The summary says N → nausea and vomiting; Na+-K+ ATPase blocker.")
finish()

